# What must NOT reach the build context.
#
# Not a mirror of .gitignore. That file answers "what should be committed"; this one
# answers "what does the running service need", and the two disagree in both
# directions - data/out/*.json is tracked and useless to the server, results/documents
# is untracked and is somebody's uploaded safety data.

# Rebuilt inside the image by `npm ci`, and copying it in is not merely wasteful: a
# host node_modules holds binaries built for the host's libc, so a native module
# compiled on a developer's machine would be copied over the one npm just installed.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# History, not runtime. Also the largest thing in the context by a wide margin.
.git/
.github/
.claude/

# The Python interpreter the upload path uses is installed in the image, from the
# pinned pymupdf below. A host virtualenv is the wrong platform's binaries and would
# shadow it if PYTHON ever pointed at a relative path.
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc

# 363MB of regulatory review PDFs, each retrievable from accessdata.fda.gov or
# ema.europa.eu by the URL the spec records. Shipping the whole corpus into every image
# layer is the wrong place to hold it.
#
# EXCEPT THE SIX THE LIBRARY ACTUALLY NAMES, and leaving them out was not "a smaller
# surface" - it was a broken one. `library.ts` reports an absent file as `askable:
# false`, and `handleDemo` attaches a document only when the source IS askable. So every
# prepared case on a deployment opened with findings and no document: Read & mark said
# "No documents on this case yet", the reader had nothing to join a finding's page to,
# and Ask answered 422 on every question. The findings cite pages in a file the
# deployment did not have.
#
# Six files, 37MB, against 363MB for the corpus - the difference between a demonstration
# that works and one that opens on an empty reader. The other twenty-nine are inputs to
# the offline evaluation and are never read by the server.
#
# Kept in step with `data/library-sources.json` by hand: a catalogue entry whose file is
# not listed here loses its document, and says so on the library page rather than
# failing quietly.
data/raw/
!data/raw/approval-packages/turalio-211810-multidiscipline.pdf
!data/raw/approval-packages/ema-epar-sample-imaavy.pdf
!data/raw/approval-packages/modern-fda-multidiscipline-211367.pdf
!data/raw/approval-packages/sotyktu-epar-assessment.pdf
!data/raw/approval-packages/tolcapone-20697-medical-review-p1.pdf
!data/raw/approval-packages/troglitazone-020720-approval.pdf

# Runtime state, not inputs. results/ holds the log, the uploaded documents, the
# account store with its password hashes, and the extraction caches. Baking any of it
# into an image would ship one deployment's data to every other one; the server mkdirs
# what it needs on boot.
results/

# Test artifacts: diagnostics for a single run.
test-results/
playwright-report/
e2e/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Built by `npm run site:build` inside the image. A stale host build copied in would be
# overwritten, but only after being copied, and only if the build succeeds.
dist/
**/dist/
.vite/
**/.vite/

# CREDENTIALS. .env holds API keys and .env.share holds a service-account blob; an
# image is copied, pushed and pulled by people who are not the person who wrote them.
# The container takes its configuration from the environment instead. .env.example is
# deliberately not listed - it is the tracked template and holds no secret.
.env
.env.local
.env.share
*.users.json

# Tuning artefacts.
apps/atmosphere/shots/
apps/landing/shots/

# Local editor and OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
